# x86/x86_64 Linux Support

Port of the MEMD kernel module to x86_64 Linux.

## Status

In progress. The module uses Linux kernel APIs common to both ARM64 and x86_64,
but requires architecture-specific adaptations for:
- Inline hooks (different calling convention, no BTI)
- Page table walk (same 4-level, different bit positions and NX bit)
- Calling convention (System V AMD64 ABI vs AAPCS64)
- KASLR slide detection (different kernel base)
- CFI (different mechanism on x86)

## Structure

```
linux/x86/
├── readme
├── kmod/          # x86_64 kernel module (ported from linux/arm/kmod)
├── client.h       # x86 userspace client (same as ARM, platform-agnostic)
├── memd.hpp       # C++ header (same as ARM)
├── run.sh         # x86 Linux loader
├── run-linux.sh   # generic Linux loader
└── Makefile       # x86 build system
```

## Key Differences from ARM64

| Feature | ARM64 | x86_64 |
|---------|-------|--------|
| Calling convention | AAPCS64 (x0-x7 args) | System V AMD64 (rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8, r9) |
| BTI landing pads | Required (bti c) | Not applicable |
| CFI | KCFI (per-function hash) | FineIBT / KCFI (different encoding) |
| Shadow call stack | x18 register | Not used (stack canary instead) |
| Page table NX bit | Bit 54 | Bit 63 |
| Kernel base | 0xFFFF800010000000 | 0xFFFFFFFF80000000 (typical) |
| Module RWX | CONFIG_STRICT_MODULE_RWX | CONFIG_STRICT_MODULE_RWX |
| Instruction cache | Separate I/D cache (flush required) | Unified cache (no flush needed) |

